.TH CHTTPSVR_STOP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_stop \- close this server's listener
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_stop(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the listener socket that chttpsvr_start registered from the
shared reactor, and closes it.  For a Unix domain socket listener it
then removes the socket file, but only while its path still names the
socket that this server created.  It returns at once.  The shared
reactor and every other registered listener keep running, and nothing
affects them.

It is idempotent.  A call to chttpsvr_stop on a server that never
started, or on one that already stopped, does nothing.

It does nothing, and says nothing, when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a
stale handle that something already destroyed.

A connection that the server accepted before this call keeps running.
The server does not close it.  A request that already runs continues
until it completes, and a keep-alive connection goes on to be served,
request after request, for as long as the client keeps it open, until
the idle timeout closes it as it would on a running server.  This is
true even across a later restart with chttpsvr_start on the same
handle, which serves such a connection under its new configuration.  chttpsvr_destroy waits for such in-flight requests to
drain completely, and closes every idle connection that remains,
before it frees the resources of the server.

It is safe to call this function with CHTTPSVR_INVALID (0).

You can call it at the same time as a chttpsvr_start call that
restarts the same handle on a different thread.  The restart waits for
the teardown of the old listener in this call to finish completely,
and then binds a new one.  It does not race it.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
This function is synchronous, and it is NOT async-signal-safe.  Do not
call it directly from a signal handler, and use
chttpsvr_engine_stop() there instead.  It takes internal locks that
the interrupted thread can already hold, for example inside
chttpsvr_start() or chttpsvr_destroy().  The calling thread can then
deadlock itself.  Use chttpsvr_engine_stop() for a shutdown hook that
a signal drives.  Only that function is documented as
async-signal-safe, because it moves all of this work off the
interrupted thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
